<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <SignAssembly>false</SignAssembly>
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The second trim canary that runs (#3965). Quartz.Trimming.Canary references Quartz alone; this one
    references the two packages of the HTTP API, and runs both halves of it in one process: Quartz.AspNetCore
    serving a scheduler over Kestrel, and Quartz.HttpClient driving that scheduler over a socket.

    What it proves that a compile cannot: the request delegates the generator wrote are the ones that run,
    the wire contract reads and writes with reflection-based System.Text.Json switched off on both sides, and
    a job named as a string in a request body comes back out as a type that runs.

    Everything else is Quartz.Trimming.Canary's, for the reasons its csproj gives. No TrimmerRootAssembly,
    no suppressions file applied, and warnings that do not stop the link: build/Build.cs reads what the
    publish reported and checks it against the ILLink.Suppressions.xml of Quartz, Quartz.AspNetCore and
    Quartz.HttpClient.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.AspNetCore\Quartz.AspNetCore.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.HttpClient\Quartz.HttpClient.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
